  "result": "`click.KeyValue` is in. The full test suite passes (1913 passed, 102 skipped, 1 xfailed), including the new tests; I didn't run a type checker.\n\n`--set a=1` now gives `(\"a\", \"1\")`. Input with no `=`, or with an empty key (like `novalue` or `=value`), exits with code 2 and the message `'novalue' is not a valid key=value pair.`\n\nBehaviours your spec didn't cover, matching the plan:\n- The value is split on the first `=`, so `a=b=c` gives `(\"a\", \"b=c\")`.\n- An empty value is allowed, so `a=` gives `(\"a\", \"\")`.\n- Spaces around the key are kept, not trimmed.\n- The empty string `\"\"` is rejected like any input without `=`.\n- If the value is already a tuple (for example a tuple default), it is passed through unchanged.\n\nWhat changed:\n- **`src/click/types.py`:** the new `KeyValue` class.\n- **`src/click/__init__.py`:** exports it as `click.KeyValue`.\n- **`docs/api.md`:** adds an entry in the Types section.\n- **`CHANGES.md`:** adds a new unreleased 8.6.0 section for it.\n- **`tests/test_types.py`:** new tests for a normal pair, a value containing `=`, an empty value, `multiple=True` turned into a `dict`, and the failure cases.\n\nI skipped the optional `tests/test_info_dict.py` entry.\n\nI haven't committed anything.",
